We do all our dumps on "live" filesystems.  I don't want to reopen the perennial
flame war about whether or not this is an OK thing to do.  (Opinion seems rather
evenly divided.  All I can say is that I've been doing it for over a decade, and
have never had any problems retrieving things from backup tapes.  Enough said.)

Anyway, I often encounter the following when dumping an OSF/1 (v1.2) filesystem:

dump: Bad read from input disk file: /dev/rrz9h, block number: -1706164224, bytes wanted: 8192, bytes got: 0
bread(): read(): No such file or directory

The first couple times it happened, I got worried, umounted the filesystem, and
fsck'ed it.  All was well.  So I assume that what's happening is that while dump
is reading from the raw device, some process(es) is/are updating files, and the
on-disk information is somehow (temporarily) inconsistent.

Sometimes there are just a couple of the above messages.  Sometimes there are a
lot (occasionally several of them seemingly issued at the same time, with parts
of several messages interspersed), and often so many that the dump is aborted.

We've never seen this problem under SunOS or IRIX.  In fact, the only time I can
recall seeing it is under 4.1 BSD.  Whatever was causing it way back then (ten or
more years ago) was fixed when 4.2 BSD came out, and I've never seen it since, in
any other flavour of UNIX.  Could it be that the OSF/1 people have reinvented the
same bug, independently?

Anyway, what I'd like to find out is whether this problem is fixed in OSF/1 v1.3
(we'll be upgrading soon).  If so, fine.  But if not, will 2.0 fix it?  If not,
why not?  (We often have jobs that run for days, or even weeks, and can't afford
to umount a filesystem just to back it up.)

