wilko@idca.tds.philips.nl (Wilko Bulte) writes:
>anthony@aaii.oz.au (Anthony Baxter) writes:
>
>>Does anyone have any ideas for a way to submit changes to DEC for system
>>man page fixes? I've fixed up proc.4 so that it formats correctly (so
>>its actually usuable!), and table.2 so it includes TBL_SWAPINFO, and it
>>would be nice if I could get someone at DEC to put this back into the
>>system so that I dont have to keep my own copy around, and remember about
>>it when upgrade time comes. 
>
>>(manpages for the system calls that arent documented would be nice, too...)
>
>I suspect that the only way to do this is file some sort of problem
>report.

Sequent boxes have (or at least did have) a nice program called
mailbug.  It allows anyone whose box has functioning email to easily
submit a bug.  It prompts people to fill in a form describing the bug,
and, before mailing off the form to Sequent, it automagically adds a
big chunk of versioning information about all the software installed
on your system (OS, compilers, etc) for the team who has to deal with
the incoming mail.

It's trivially easy to submit a bug, and the machine does all the hard
work (describing your setup) for you.  Furthermore, any user can
submit a bug; you don't need to have a support contract -- and for
things like man page errors, I don't need any response from the
company, as long as it gets fixed.

Including a program like this with your OS is a way to _dramatically_
increase the stability & quality of your product.  I say this because,
as a UNIX system administrator, I constantly find bugs of all sorts in
many parts of the systems I run.  As it is, I don't have time to
submit them to the vendor unless they are real show-stoppers.

Having to root around in your files to figure out the various contract
info; having to sit through long calls with some support person who
may or may not have a clue; being unable to (easily) submit logs of
the problem; having to guess at which configuration info might be
relevant... make it not worth my time unless the bug is directly in my
way.

Having a mailbug like program on all the boxes I support would be a
huge win, and I would submit many more bug reports than I do now.  In
the interest of higher quality systems for everyone, I would like to
beg the representatives from the vendors who are reading this to try
and convince their organizations to start including a mailbug-like a
program with every machine they ship.

This doesn't need to interfere with vendors' support programs, as a
very large percentage of the bugs I don't need support for at all; I'd
just like to seem them fixed in the next OS rev.  A judicious message
at the top saying something along the lines of "customers without a
support contract can expect a status message within a month" might
help out here.

A final argument for doing so: Sequent has managed to make this work
for them, and it has resulted in (I think) happier customers (though I
may be biased, having spent a few summers working there).  It could do
the same for your firm as well.

Note that follow-ups have been redirected to comp.unix.misc.
-- 
Dan Mosedale, Systems Admin            Email: mosedale@genome.Stanford.EDU
Stanford Genetics Department


