I've done a very simple 'benchmark' on our Alpha's (3K/400 OSF1 1.2) to 
see how pthreads/cmathreads compared to standard processes with regard 
to context switching.

Gist of the thing:

  create two threads or processes, alternate executing (essentially) 

  gettimeofday(&start, NULL);  /* actually occurs only once */

  for(;;) {
    if (++count >= 10000) 
	break;

    [cma/pthread/sched]_yield(); /* as appropriate */
  }
  gettimeofday(&stop, NULL);
 
  dump the equivalent of (stop - start)

 Not much to it really.


The results were not what I expected. Basically the processes were
consistently faster:

 abstraction   avg time (usec) to execute 
   cmathread     199104
   pthread       192272
   process       164944
	
   control          976  (single thread executing for loop w/o switching)

   (note that the resolution of the timer is 976 usec)

I had expected threads to be the faster. My feeling is that either I've 
made an invalid assumption about the benchmark or all three abstractions 
somehow map to the same Mach abstraction and processes are more direct 
about it.

Comments?


Profiling shows the following (digested, each for 1 thread/process, 
 BTW: anyway to get statistics for ALL threads?)

cmathreads
               cycl/
 calls %cycles  call sec   procedure
 5000   36.22    12  .0004 cma_yield (blah/DECthreads/COMMON/cma_thread.c)
 5000    9.05     3  .0001 swtch_pri (blah/alpha/usr/include/mach/syscall_sw.h)
    2   30.21 50000  .0003 (benchmark procedure)

pthreads
               cycl/
 calls %cycles  call sec   procedure
 5000   36.85    12 .0004 pthread_yield (blah/DECthreads/COMMON/cma_thread.c)
 5000    9.21     3 .0001 swtch_pri (blah/alpha/usr/include/mach/syscall_sw.h)
    2   30.73 50038       (benchmark procedure)

unix process
               cycl/
 calls %cycles  call  sec   procedure
 4999   21.34       3 .0001 sched_yield (blah/librt/alpha/schyield.s)
    1   78.27   55009 .0004 (benchmark procedure)


swtch_pri apparently traps to the kernel (eg, .../mach/syscall_sw.h)
It makes sense that sched_yield does the same.
I cant decide whether cma/pthread_yield are just simple library 
routines or another trap stub.

I thought OSF/1 threads were a user-level implementation, is this incorrect?

Any feedback anyone wishes to offer is appreciated.

Thanks
