          DECUS '93/SAN FRANCISCO TO FEATURE WINDOWS NT SPOTLIGHT

           Interactive Peer-To-Peer Knowledge Sets Program Apart 
                          From Other NT Offerings


A new spotlight on Microsoft Windows NT at DECUS '93/San Francisco will give 
attendees expert knowledge on how to integrate this emerging technology into 
a multiplatform environment. The program is sponsored by the U.S. Chapter of 
the Digital Equipment Computer Users Society (DECUS), and is part of the 
Society's semiannual national event.

What makes this Windows NT program unique is the interactive peer-to-peer 
knowledge gained from fellow attendees during question and answer periods, 
Birds of a Feather meetings and informal discussions.

"There's never been a stronger NT program," said Donald Fuhr, DECUS National 
Event Technical Focus chair. "We've gathered the top experts and created 
timely, in-depth sessions and seminars. But what really sets this apart from 
other NT programs is the DECUS environment. Attendees not only learn from 
the presenters, they get a competitive edge from fellow attendees who have a 
strong background in OpenVMS and are personally familiar with implementing 
the style of computing on which NT was based. No one else offers this 
benefit."

The in-depth Windows NT program was developed to address the needs of three 
audiences: managers trying to understand where Windows NT fits into their 
computing strategy, migration planning, etc.; developers and programmers 
implementing solutions based on Windows NT as well as deploying existing 
applications to Windows NT; and system managers responsible for integrating 
Windows NT into their Digital environment and managing networks of Windows 
NT workstations. 

The Windows NT program will be held from December 8 - 10, and is chaired by 
David A. Solomon, a leading expert in the Windows NT field and a well known 
speaker in the DECUS community. Solomon will also lead a full-day seminar 
and a session during the program. Additional Windows NT seminars will also 
be presented on December 4 and 5.

"This year DECUS attendees will have the added benefit of attending a high 
quality conference-within-a-conference targeting a hot market - Windows NT," 
said Solomon. "This program will include a mix of Microsoft, Digital and 
independent industry experts, providing attendees with a balanced view of 
Windows NT. In addition, people will also have access to invaluable DECUS 
resources such as the trade show, networking with peers and regular DECUS 
sessions."

Windows NT Program Features

The Windows NT program will include a keynote address, four streams of 35 
technical sessions and five all-day seminars -- all led by experts in the 
Windows NT world.

The program's 35 high quality Windows NT technical sessions will be held 
December 8 - 9. Topics range from "Mixed Network Client/Server Computing 
with Windows NT" and "Integrating Windows NT in a PATHWORKS Environment," to 
"Visual C++ for Windows NT" and "Porting OpenVMS & UNIX Applications to 
Windows NT."  

Additionally, on December 10, attendees can choose from one of five all-day 
seminars to round out their Windows NT experience. These seminars include: 

 Windows NT Technical Description in OpenVMS Terms 
 Windows NT Technical Description in UNIX Terms 
 Win32 System Architecture:  Capabilities, Features and Fundamentals 
 Visual C++/NT Programming Tutorial 
 Windows NT System Management & Basic Networking

Participants of the Windows NT program will also receive admission to the 
DECUS trade show from December 7 - 9, and can attend the Digital Technology 
Center.

Windows NT Program Speakers

Sessions and seminars will be presented by a mix of Digital, Microsoft and 
industry experts in the Windows NT field.

The Windows NT program keynote address will be delivered by Jesse Lipcon, 
vice president of Windows NT at Digital Equipment Corporation, on Wednesday, 
December 8 at 9:00 a.m. Lipcon will speak about Windows NT and the Future of 
Digital. He is responsible for Windows NT technical and business strategy, 
development and marketing, as well as Digital's relationship with Microsoft.

Additional speakers include:

Darryl Havens, the Windows NT I/O system architect and original member of 
the Windows NT development group at Microsoft.  

Douglas Hamilton, president of Hamilton Laboratories and author of the 
popular Hamilton C shell, a highly multi-threaded tools package for Windows 
NT, designed from scratch to recreate the functionality of the original UNIX 
C shell and utilities.  

Jeffrey Richter, author of Advanced Windows NT Programming and Windows 3.1: 
A Developer's Guide, contributing editor to Microsoft Systems Journal and 
developer of the DeMystifiers, a suite of 16-bit Windows developer tools.  

Richard Hale Shaw, editor of NT Developer Journal, the only technical 
journal devoted solely to professional NT programmers, and contributing 
editor to PC Magazine, Windows Tech Journal and Microsoft Systems Journal.  

Paul Yao, co-author of the first book ever published on Windows programming 
(1986) called Programmers Guide to Windows, and author of the leading 
tutorial on Windows programming, Peter Norton's Windows 3.1 Power 
Programming Techniques.  

A two-day seminar focusing on Windows NT Kernal-Mode Device Drivers for 
OpenVMS Writers, will be presented on December 4 and 5. The seminar, which 
focuses on porting attendees' experience in writing OpenVMS device drivers 
to the Windows NT environment, will be lead by Jamie Hanrahan, a respected 
consultant specializing in application design, internals, device drivers and 
network programming under OpenVMS.

An Introduction to Windows NT Architecture and Management will be held on 
December 4. The seminar will be presented by Marshall Wilinsky of Lotus 
Development Corporation and Candace Reiden of Digital Equipment Corporation.

On Sunday, December 5, Digital Learning Services will present Domain 
Management in a Windows NT Advanced Server Environment. Deanne Lickers, an 
educational consultant at the DLS Training Center will lead this seminar.

The Windows NT offerings will be presented during DECUS '93/San Francisco, 
which runs from Saturday, December 4, 1993 through Friday, December 10, 1993 
at the Moscone Convention Center in San Francisco. DECUS '93/San Francisco 
provides the most comprehensive knowledge experience in the computer 
industry, with over 100 seminars, 1,100 symposium sessions, 125+ 
multiplatform computing vendors and a large educational display of Digital 
Equipment Corporation technology.  

Registration

Participants who register by the early-bird deadline, November 15, will 
receive a discount on registration fees. To register early or for further 
information, interested participants should call 1-800-DECUS55.

