In article <2b3iddINNui4@rs18.hrz.th-darmstadt.de>,
Gerd Aschemann <ascheman@isa.informatik.th-darmstadt.de> wrote:
>Hi,
>
>we have problems with swap space on our AXP 3000/400 (96 M RAM/212 M
>Swap, OSF1 1.3). The memory usage always grows and NEVER shrinks.
>Especially the X-Server had a size of 135 M after some days. After
>rebooting, X now has 11 M with the first user logged on. Now the
>kernel (Process 0) has 292 M. But swap seems to be normal.
>
>Has anyone had the same problems. What can we do? Is it a bug in
>/usr/bin/X or in the kernel???

Neither, it's just the way it works.  The X server gets memory for all
sorts of things as clients make requests, but when the memory is freed,
it isn't given back to the system for use.

Put the following line in your /usr/lib/X11/xdm/xdm-config file, send xdm a
HUP signal, and it will restart the X server when users log out. 

DisplayManager*terminateServer: true


