Mike Coleman (coleman@sparc1.cstp.umkc.edu) wrote:
: mferrare@physics.adelaide.edu.au (Mark Ferraretto) writes:
: |How do you tell how much swap you have left under OSF/1  I have an alpha
: |500 that keeps on saying reached 10% of swap space or something like that.
: |There is  no pstat command so how do I do it?

: Say swapon -s.  You don't need to be root.

: Note that on a DEC OSF machine as shipped, only about 10% of your swap
: partition is usable.  Or, to put it another way, you need about ten times as
: much swap space as you would need on a typical Unix box in order to support
: the same amount of activity.

	Well, after some research, I found some info. Actually, it is explained
	in the OSF manual System Admin chapter 7 files and file systems.

	It would seem OSF on the Alpha (everywhere?) can do swapping two 
	different ways. The default one (at least on our machine), the OS
	reserves a page of swap for every modified page in memory, even if no
	swapping is been done. This caused our 64Mb mach to run out of 84Mb swap
	for the simplest of operations (like a compile), when it wasn't even
	swapping yet. You just run out of space to reserve.

	The second way is to go get a page of swap WHEN YOU NEED TO SWAP.
	The danger implied is that, under heavy load, if you need to swap and
	you ran out of swap, that process might die.

	So, we eliminated a link from /sbin/swapdefault to the swap device
	and rebooted. With the link gone, you run under method #2. With the
	link there, you run method #1. After this, we were able to compile
	programs, run Windows, etc, etc without the 10% deal...

	Off course, you assume responsability for not running out of swap...

	Hope this helps.

