>"Es fehlt immer ein Minimum, um das Maximum zu erreichen" W.Falin


by now many people have told you about how the X server allocates memory and
never really free's it back to unix. That's just the way unix works, but there
are other problems to do with memory allocation (via malloc).

Malloc (at least under OSF 1.2 - DEC haven't sent me 1.3 dispite *many* 
requests) appears to round up the size of your malloc request to the next
power of 2. We found (to our dismay) that a program malloc'ing 32Mb+1b
would grow by 64Mb! Alarming at the extreme, but possibly also a cumulative
memory hog when you are doing *lots* of small size mallocs. It may well be
that your X server is really only using about half (more or less) the memory
it really needs!

